    {
      "heading": "Reading without vowel marks",
      "text": [
        "Short vowels (a, i, u) can be shown with small marks above and below the letters, but everyday Arabic — books, newspapers, signs, phones — leaves them out, and so does this app. The pronunciation under each word gives them.",
        "Doubled consonants are not shown either: سكر is sukkar, sugar.",
        "Without vowels, different words can share a spelling, and context tells them apart: رجل is rajul (man) or rijl (leg); من is man (who) or min (from); أنت is anta or anti (you, to a man or a woman).",
        "ال is \"the\", written joined to its word. Before t, th, d, dh, r, z, s, sh, ṣ, ḍ, ṭ, ẓ, l and n (the \"sun letters\") the l is not said and the next consonant doubles: السبت as-sabt, Saturday."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Reading rules",
      "text": [
        "The pronunciations show each word as said on its own, without the grammatical endings of formal reading: كتاب [kiˈtaːb], not kitaabun.",
        "Stress falls on the last syllable if it ends in a long vowel plus a consonant (كتاب [kiˈtaːb]); otherwise on the second-to-last if that syllable is long, otherwise on the one before (مدرسة [ˈmadrasa]).",
        "The emphatic letters ص ض ط ظ (and ق) darken a neighboring a toward the a in \"father\": طاولة [ˈtˤɑːwila]."
      ]
    },
